      "text": "To find the length of the hypotenuse \\( h \\) of the right triangle with legs \\(\\log_4 27\\) and \\(\\log_2 9\\), we first need to convert these logarithms to a common base. We will use the change of base formula for logarithms, which states that \\(\\log_4 27 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 4}\\) and \\(\\log_2 9 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 4}\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate \\(\\log_2 27\\):\n\\[\n\\log_2 27 = \\log_2 (3^3) = 3 \\log_2 3.\n\\]\nNext, we know \\(\\log_2 4 = \\log_2 (2^2) = 2\\). Therefore,\n\\[\n\\log_4 27 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 4} = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\nSimilarly,\n\\[\n\\log_2 9 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 4} = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\nThe length of the hypotenuse \\( h \\) is given by the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{(\\log_4 27)^2 + (\\log_2 9)^2} = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2} = \\sqrt{2 \\left( \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2} = \\sqrt{2 \\cdot \\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{2}} = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\]\nNow, we need to compute \\( 4^h \\):\n\\[\n4^h = 4^{\\frac{3 \\log_2 3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}} = (2^2)^{\\frac{3 \\log_2 3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}} = 2^{2 \\cdot \\frac{3 \\log_2 3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}} = 2^{3 \\log_2 3 \\sqrt{2}} = (2^{\\log_2 3 \\sqrt{2}})^3 = (3 \\sqrt{2})^3 = 27 \\cdot 2 \\sqrt{2} = 54 \\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{54\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]",
